import{_ as t,c as s,o,ag as a}from"./chunks/framework.DZ_cNctr.js";const f=JSON.parse('{"title":"Zeiss CZI files","description":"Open a Zeiss CZI file from ZEN by its web address, an s3:// address or from your disk. Z-stacks, time series, scenes, mosaics, stored pyramids and Celldiscoverer 7 plates open without converting anything.","frontmatter":{"title":"Zeiss CZI files","description":"Open a Zeiss CZI file from ZEN by its web address, an s3:// address or from your disk. Z-stacks, time series, scenes, mosaics, stored pyramids and Celldiscoverer 7 plates open without converting anything."},"headers":[],"relativePath":"guides/zeiss-czi.md","filePath":"guides/zeiss-czi.md"}'),i={name:"guides/zeiss-czi.md"};function r(n,e,l,d,h,p){return o(),s("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[a(`<h1 id="zeiss-czi-files" tabindex="-1">Zeiss CZI files <a class="header-anchor" href="#zeiss-czi-files" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Zeiss CZI files&quot;">​</a></h1><p>Find Nuclei Viewer opens Zeiss CZI files, the format ZEN writes. Nothing is converted and nothing is uploaded. The viewer reads the file by byte range and decodes only the pieces you look at, so a large file starts showing before it is downloaded.</p><p>Other formats the viewer opens are listed in <a href="./supported-formats.html">Supported Formats</a>. Plates from other instruments have their own guides: <a href="./opera-phenix-plates.html">Opera Phenix Plates</a> and <a href="./yokogawa-plates.html">Yokogawa CellVoyager Plates</a>.</p><h2 id="opening-a-czi-from-the-web" tabindex="-1">Opening a CZI from the web <a class="header-anchor" href="#opening-a-czi-from-the-web" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Opening a CZI from the web&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Click <strong>Open Image</strong> on the landing page, paste the address of the <code>.czi</code> file into the URL field and click <strong>Load from URL</strong>. An <code>https://</code> address and an <code>s3://</code> address both work.</p><div class="language- vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang"></span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span>https://zenodo.org/api/records/7015307/files/W96_B2+B4_S=2_T=1=Z=1_C=1_Tile=5x9.czi/content</span></span>
<span class="line"><span></span></span>
<span class="line"><span>s3://your-bucket/your-folder/your-file.czi</span></span></code></pre></div><p>The viewer reads the start of the file, then the index of the file, then the pixels. It never downloads the whole file to open it.</p><h2 id="opening-a-czi-from-disk" tabindex="-1">Opening a CZI from disk <a class="header-anchor" href="#opening-a-czi-from-disk" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Opening a CZI from disk&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Drop the <code>.czi</code> file on the viewer. A folder of CZI files works too: see <a href="#plates-saved-as-one-file-per-field">Plates saved as one file per field</a>. Or click <strong>Open Image</strong>, go to the <strong>Local Files</strong> tab and click <strong>Choose File</strong>. This works in every browser, Chrome, Edge, Firefox and Safari alike, because a CZI is one file. Nothing leaves your computer.</p><h2 id="what-opens" tabindex="-1">What opens <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-opens" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What opens&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>Z-stacks, time series and channels.</strong> The viewer shows every plane and moves through Z and T. Z-stacks open in 3D too.</li><li><strong>Scenes.</strong> A file with several scenes opens as a series. Pick a scene from the <strong>Series</strong> dropdown.</li><li><strong>Mosaics.</strong> Tiled images are stitched on the fly, at the positions ZEN recorded.</li><li><strong>Stored pyramids.</strong> When ZEN saved lower resolutions, the viewer uses them. Zooming out stays fast.</li><li><strong>Celldiscoverer 7 and other well plates.</strong> A CZI with a plate layout opens in the plate view, with the well navigator and the well picker. See <a href="./hcs-plates.html">HCS Plates</a>.</li><li><strong>Compression.</strong> Uncompressed files and Zstd files open.</li><li><strong>Pixel types.</strong> 8-bit and 16-bit grayscale, and 8-bit and 16-bit RGB.</li></ul><p>Channel colours come from ZEN&#39;s display settings, the same colours you see in ZEN. When a file has no pixel size, it opens at scale 1 and the Image Information dialog says so.</p><h2 id="plates" tabindex="-1">Plates <a class="header-anchor" href="#plates" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Plates&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A plate in a CZI stores each field at one resolution. So, like an Opera Phenix plate, it opens in Field view only. The note under the Field | Well | Plate toggle says so, and <strong>Learn more</strong> opens this guide. Want plate and well overviews? Convert the file to OME-Zarr. The <a href="./opera-phenix-plates.html#why-there-is-no-plate-or-well-view">Opera Phenix guide</a> explains why.</p><p>The viewer follows the plate rules of <a href="https://github.com/sebi06/czitools" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">czitools</a>. When a file has scenes but no usable plate layout, it opens as a series of scenes and the info line says why.</p><p>An Example Gallery tile, <strong>Zeiss CZI</strong>, opens a real plate with one click. Plates saved as one file per field are <a href="#plates-saved-as-one-file-per-field">described below</a>.</p><h2 id="plates-saved-as-one-file-per-field" tabindex="-1">Plates saved as one file per field <a class="header-anchor" href="#plates-saved-as-one-file-per-field" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Plates saved as one file per field&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Some instruments write every field of a plate to its own CZI file. <a href="https://idr.openmicroscopy.org" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">IDR</a> idr0011 is an example. The viewer puts those files back together as one plate, in the plate view.</p><p><strong>From a folder.</strong> Drop the folder on the viewer. Or click <strong>Open Image</strong>, go to the <strong>Local Files</strong> tab and click <strong>Choose Data Folder</strong>. Folders open in Chrome and Edge. The plate opens in Field view, with the well navigator and the well picker. In Firefox and Safari, use the zip.</p><p><strong>From a zip.</strong> Zip the folder without compression, then drop the <code>.zip</code> on the viewer, click <strong>Choose File</strong> on the <strong>Local Files</strong> tab, or paste its web address. <strong>Choose File</strong> is for a single file or a zip. <strong>Choose Data Folder</strong> is for a folder. The viewer reads the zip by byte range, so it never downloads all of it.</p><div class="language- vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang"></span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span>zip -0 -r plate.zip folder</span></span></code></pre></div><p>The <code>-0</code> matters. It stores the files as they are, which lets the viewer read each CZI in parts. A compressed zip is refused with a message that says so.</p><p><strong>How the plate is built.</strong> Wells come from the metadata in each file, never from the file name. Fields in a well follow the time they were acquired. Wells may hold different numbers of fields, and each well shows only its own.</p><p><strong>One folder, several things.</strong> When a folder or zip holds more than one thing, a list titled <strong>Choose a dataset</strong> appears. Each row is one thing. Pick a row to open it.</p><ul><li><strong>A plate.</strong> The row shows how many fields and wells it holds.</li><li><strong>An image.</strong> A single file. When a file is set apart from its plate, its row says why. For example: its channels, size, Z, T or pixel size differ from the rest of the plate; it has the same well and position as another file, taken later; its well lies outside the plate; or its well name does not match its row and column.</li><li><strong>A file that cannot be read.</strong> The row is greyed out and shows the reason, such as &quot;Not a CZI file.&quot; or &quot;Part of a split file.&quot;</li></ul><p>A folder with exactly one thing opens without the list.</p><p><strong>One field alone.</strong> Open a single field file and it opens as one field. The viewer tells you: &quot;This file is one field of a plate. Drop the whole folder to see the plate.&quot;</p><p>Like every CZI plate, these open in Field view only. See <a href="#plates">Plates</a> above.</p><h2 id="what-does-not-open-yet" tabindex="-1">What does not open yet <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-does-not-open-yet" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What does not open yet&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A file the viewer cannot read is refused with a message that names the file and says what to do. These are the messages.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Case</th><th>Message</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>JPEG XR</td><td>This CZI file is compressed with JPEG XR. The viewer cannot read JPEG XR yet. In ZEN, save a copy without compression or with Zstd, and open that.</td></tr><tr><td>JPG, LZW or another compression</td><td>This CZI file uses a compression the viewer cannot read yet (JPG). In ZEN, save a copy without compression or with Zstd, and open that.</td></tr><tr><td>32-bit float and other pixel types</td><td>This CZI file stores Gray32Float pixels. The viewer reads 8-bit, 16-bit and RGB images.</td></tr><tr><td>Channels with different pixel types</td><td>This CZI file mixes pixel types across channels (Gray8, Gray16). The viewer cannot show them together yet.</td></tr><tr><td>A large mosaic with no stored pyramid</td><td>This scene is a large tiled image with no lower resolutions stored, so the viewer cannot show it yet. In ZEN, save a copy with a pyramid, and open that.</td></tr><tr><td>An extra dimension, such as rotation or phase</td><td>This CZI file has more than one phase (2). The viewer cannot show that dimension yet.</td></tr><tr><td>A file still open in ZEN</td><td>This CZI file was not closed by ZEN, so it may be incomplete. Open it in ZEN and save it again.</td></tr><tr><td>A file split into parts</td><td>This CZI file is split into parts. The viewer opens single-file CZI only.</td></tr><tr><td>A zip with compressed CZI files</td><td>{zip}: The CZI files in this zip are compressed, so they cannot be read in parts. Make the zip without compression (for example zip -0 -r plate.zip folder) and open that.</td></tr><tr><td>A very large index</td><td>This CZI file is too large to open without an index. An index for large files is planned.</td></tr><tr><td>Not a CZI</td><td>This file is not a CZI file.</td></tr></tbody></table><p><strong>Saving a copy in ZEN.</strong> Most refusals share one fix. In ZEN, save a copy of the file without compression or with Zstd. Open that copy. For a big mosaic, save it with a pyramid.</p><p><strong>Big mosaics.</strong> A tiled scene wider or taller than 3000 pixels needs stored lower resolutions. Without them the viewer would have to read every tile to draw one screen. A scene with a single tile per plane always opens.</p><p><strong>Other tools.</strong> If you use <a href="https://www.openmicroscopy.org/bio-formats/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Bio-Formats</a>, it reads JPEG XR and converts a CZI to OME-Zarr. The <a href="https://www.find-nuclei.com/blog/how-to-convert-to-ome-zarr" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">conversion guide</a> shows how. Large mosaics, such as Axioscan slide scans, need the stored pyramid described above.</p><h2 id="hosts" tabindex="-1">Hosts <a class="header-anchor" href="#hosts" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Hosts&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Opening by address needs a server that answers byte-range requests and allows them through <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/CORS" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">CORS</a>. Most public data servers do. These are tested:</p><ul><li><strong>Zenodo, API links.</strong> Use the form <code>https://zenodo.org/api/records/&lt;id&gt;/files/&lt;name&gt;/content</code>. It works.</li><li><strong>Zenodo, download links.</strong> A link that ends in <code>?download=1</code> does not. The server sends no CORS headers, so a browser cannot read it. Switch to the API form above.</li><li><strong>BioImage Archive.</strong> Files on <code>ftp.ebi.ac.uk</code> over https work.</li><li><strong>Your own bucket.</strong> Allow <code>GET</code> and the <code>Range</code> header in the bucket&#39;s CORS rules. <a href="./remote-urls.html">Remote URLs</a> has the details.</li></ul><p>When a server cannot send parts of a file, the viewer says so. Download the file and open it from your disk.</p><p>A public server that rate-limits requests may slow down a large mosaic, because each tile is a request.</p><h2 id="privacy" tabindex="-1">Privacy <a class="header-anchor" href="#privacy" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Privacy&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The viewer reads the file in your browser. Opening a CZI counts as one opened image of the kind &quot;Zeiss CZI file&quot;. The file name and address are never recorded. See the <a href="https://find-nuclei.github.io/privacy.html" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">privacy policy</a>.</p>`,41)])])}const g=t(i,[["render",r]]);export{f as __pageData,g as default};
